{"surah": 112, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ", "translation": "Say, \"He is Allāh, [who is] One,[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَحَدٌ", "translit": "ٌdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ ", "translation": "Allāh, the Eternal Refuge.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلصَّمَدُ", "translit": "ُdَmَّṣlٱ", "root": "صمد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ ", "translation": "He neither begets nor is born,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَلِدْ", "translit": "ْdِlَy", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُولَدْ", "translit": "ْdَlwُy", "root": "ولد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ ", "translation": "Nor is there to Him any equivalent.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَكُن", "translit": "nُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُفُوًا", "translit": "āًwُfُk", "root": "كفا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَحَدٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "وفي الصبح في السفر قل يا أيها الكافرون وقل هو الله أحد وكذلك في ركعتي الفجر والطواف والتحية وهو في جميع ذلك مستديم للقيام ووضع اليدين كما وصفنا في أول الصلاة الركوع ولواحقه ثم يركع ويراعي فيه أمورا وهو أن يكبر للركوع وأن يرفع يديه مع تكبيرة الركوع وأن يمد التكبير مدا إلى الانتهاء إلى الركوع وأن يضع راحتيه على ركبتيه في الركوع وأصابعه منشورة موجهة نحو القبلة على طول الساق وأن ينصب ركبتيه ولا يثنيهما وأن يمد ظهره مستويا وأن يكون عنقه ورأسه مستويين مع ظهره كالصفيحة الواحدة لا يكون رأسه أخف", "english": "I say: Then the worshipper begins with the opening supplication (duʿāʾ al-istiftāḥ). It is excellent that he say, following his utterance of \"Allāhu akbar, Allāhu akbar\": \"Kabīran wa-l-ḥamdu lillāhi kathīran wa-subḥāna Allāhi bukratan wa-aṣīlā\" (Great indeed is Allah, and abundant praise be to Allah, and glory be to Allah morning and evening) {«الله أكبر كبيرا والحمد لله كثيرا وسبحان الله بكرة وأصيلا»} — then: \"Wajjahtu wajhiya...\" (I have turned my face...) up to his saying \"...wa-anā min al-muslimīn\" (and I am among those who submit) {«وجهت وجهي إلى قوله وأنا من المسلمين»}. Then he says: \"Subḥānaka Allāhumma wa-bi-ḥamdika wa-tabāraka-smuka wa-taʿālā jadduka wa-jalla thanāʾuka wa-lā ilāha ghayruk\" (Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your Name and exalted is Your majesty; glorious is Your praise and there is no god but You) {«سبحانك اللهم وبحمدك وتبارك اسمك وتعالى جدك وجل ثناؤك ولا إله غيرك»} — so that he may combine within himself the various dispersed reports transmitted in the traditions.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 127", "section_title_en": "Section 127 — On the Recitation (Qirāʾah) and the Bowing (Rukūʿ) with Its Attendant Matters", "section_index": 127, "anchors": "- Root: قرأ (Qaraʾa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gathering and assembling letters into articulated speech; recitation as the oral articulation of written or memorized text.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic and liturgical context, qirāʾah is the deliberate, worshipful utterance of the divine word, distinct from mere reading; it is an act of devotion requiring presence of heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes the physical articulation of sounds; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to the inward recitation of the heart in contemplation.\n- Root: ركع (Rakaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The physical act of bowing; the bending of the back in humility.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء، فتارة يستعمل في الهيئة المخصوصة في الصلاة كما هي، وتارة في التواضع والتذلل\" — The bowing is an inclination, used sometimes for the specific posture in prayer, and sometimes for humility and self-abasement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" — an old man bowed with age. Metaphorical (majāz): \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" — camels fatigued until they lowered their heads, indicating the physical posture as the outward sign of inward submission.\n- Root: صبح (Ṣabaḥa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الصبح والصباح، أول النهار\" — the dawn is the beginning of the day, the time when the horizon reddens.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"أصبح يا رجل: انتبه من غفلتك\" — \"become awake, O man: awaken from your heedlessness,\" showing the dawn prayer as the time of spiritual awakening.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between governing and governed elements, applied here to the proper pausing (waqf) and connecting (waṣl) of Quranic phrases to preserve meaning.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "فتارة يتعرف إليهم بالتقديس فيقول قل هو الله أحد الله الصمد لم يلد ولم يولد ولم يكن له كفوا أحد وتارة يتعرف إليهم بصفات جلاله فيقول الملك القدوس السلام المؤمن المهيمن العزيز الجبار المتكبر وتارة يتعرف إليهم في أفعاله المخوفة والمرجوة فيتلو عليهم سنته في أعدائه وفي أنبيائه فيقول ألم تر كيف فعل ربك بعاد إرم ذات العماد ألم تر كيف فعل ربك بأصحاب الفيل ولايعدو القرآن هذه الأقسام الثلاثة وهي الإرشاد إلى معرفة ذات الله وتقديسه أو معرفة صفاته وأسمائه أو معرفة أفعاله وسنته مع عباده ولما اشتملت سورة الإخلاص على أحد ه", "english": "And in the report [it is said]: Verily God—exalted is He—revealed to a servant whom He had taken by the hand after he had been on the brink of destruction: \"How many a sin did you commit against Me, and I forgave it for you—sins for the sake of which I destroyed entire nations among the nations!\" So this is the way (sunnah) of God—exalted is He—with His servants, by way of favor (tafḍīl), and bringing forward (taqdīm), and deferring (taʾkhīr), according to what the eternal will (al-mashīʾah al-azaliyyah) had previously decreed.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 1188", "section_title_en": "On the Meaning of Contentment with God's Decree (Al-Riḍā bi Qaḍāʾ Allāh), Its Reality, and What Has Been Reported Concerning Its Virtue", "section_index": 1188, "anchors": "- Root: رضي (Raḍā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of being pleased, satisfied, consenting; the opposite of anger (sakhaṭ). Its object is that which is agreeable and harmonious with one's will.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Raḍā is a state of the heart (ḥāl min aḥwāl al-qalb) signifying the soul's inclination toward what is pleasing, and its cessation of opposition. In theological terms, it is the soul's acquiescence to the divine decree (qaḍāʾ) after the removal of inner resistance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"رَضِيَ عَنْهُ\" — to be content with someone. Metaphorical (majāz): \"رَضِيَ بِالكَوْنِ\" — submission to what is, even when it opposes the appetites, which is the higher spiritual usage.\n- Root: قضي (Qaḍā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To decree, determine, execute, or finish a matter; the cutting off of a thing decisively.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qaḍāʾ is the divine determination (taqdīr) and execution of affairs according to prior eternal knowledge; it encompasses both what is feared and what is hoped for.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the completion of a judgment. Metaphorical: the divine decree as the all-encompassing will of God, which the believer must embrace.\n- Root: ولي (Waliya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness, guardianship, and governance; the one who manages affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Walī is the one who is near to God through obedience, and God is the Walī of the believer through grace and protection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: proximity. Metaphorical: friendship and divine patronage.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Predicate Emphasis (Bāb al-Taʾkīd bi al-Ḍamīr al-Munfaṣil) — The repetition of the pronoun for emphasis, as in \"اللهُ هُوَ\" structures, to affirm the exclusivity of divine attributes without ambiguity.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}], "3": [{"arabic": "القيوم إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا العلي العظيم إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا لم ألد ولم أولد إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا العفو الغفور إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا مبدىء كل شيء وإلي يعود العزيز الحكيم الرحمن الرحيم مالك يوم الدين خالق الخير والشر خالق الجنة والنار الواحد الأحد الفرد الصمد الذي لم يتخذ صاحبة ولا ولدا الفرد الوتر عالم الغيب والشهادة الملك القدوس السلام المؤمن المهيمن العزيز الجبار المتكبر الخالق البارىء المصور الكبير المتعال المقتدر القهار الحليم الكريم أهل الثناء والمجد أعلم السر وأخفى القادر الرزاق فوق الخلق والخل", "english": "Muḥammad ibn Ḥassān said: Maʿrūf al-Karkhī — may God have mercy on him — said to me: \"Shall I not teach you ten words — five for this world and five for the Hereafter? Whoever supplicates God — Mighty and Majestic — with them shall find God — Most High — present with them.\" I said: \"Write them down for me.\" He said: \"No, but I shall repeat them to you as Bakr ibn Khunays — may God have mercy on him — repeated them to me:", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 249", "section_title_en": "Section 249 — Supplications of the Righteous (Adʿiyat al-Ṣāliḥīn)", "section_index": 249, "anchors": "- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of counting, reckoning, and sufficiency — \"ḥasb\" denotes that which suffices or is enough\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحساب: استعمال العدد\" — the theological nuance of ḥasbī Allāh (\"God is my Sufficer\") carries the sense of complete reliance upon God's reckoning and provision, transcending mere numerical counting to signify total trust in divine sufficiency\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"حسب المال\" (he counted the wealth); Metaphorical (majāz): \"خرجا يتحسبان الأخبار\" (they go out seeking news) — in the supplication \"ḥasbī Allāh,\" the literal sense of \"sufficiency\" is intended, yet it carries the metaphorical extension of complete trust, as one who has God as his Sufficer has no need of any other reckoning\n\n- Root: اله (ʾ-l-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of worship, devotion, and seeking refuge; \"ilāh\" denotes that which is worshipped\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الله: قيل: أصله إله فحذفت همزته، وأدخل عليها الألف واللام، فخص بالباري تعالى\" — the name Allāh is the proper name of the Creator, distinguished from generic \"ilāh\" (any object of worship); in these supplications, the divine names invoked (al-Karīm, al-Ḥalīm, al-Qawī, al-Shadīd, al-Raḥīm, al-Raʾūf, al-Laṭīf, al-Qadīr) each specify particular attributes of the divine essence\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فلان يتأله: يتعبد\" (he worships); the supplications employ the divine names in their literal theological sense, each name invoking a specific attribute relevant to the particular need mentioned\n\n- Root: انا (ʾ-n-y)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أنا ضمير المخبر عن نفسه\" — the first-person pronoun; in the divine self-declaration \"innī anā Allāh,\" the repetition emphasizes divine self-identification\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أنى الطعام أي إدراكه\" (the food ripened/reached its time) — the related sense of something reaching its appointed time appears in \"عند المسألة في القبر\" (at the questioning in the grave), indicating appointed moments of divine reckoning\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The supplications employ repeated prepositional phrases (ʿinda al-mawt, ʿinda al-masʾalah, ʿinda al-ḥisāb, ʿinda al-mīzān, ʿinda al-ṣirāṭ) in parallel construction, each governing a distinct divine name, creating a rhythmic liturgical structure where each clause stands as an independent invocation\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم أعيذك بالله الأحد الصمد الذي لم يلد ولم يولد ولم يكن له كفوا أحد من شر ما تجد قالها مرارا (6) ودخل صلى الله عليه وسلم على علي بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه وهو مريض فقال له قل اللهم إني أسألك تعجيل عافيتك أو صبرا على بليتك أو خروجا من الدنيا إلى رحمتك فإنك ستعطي إحداهن (7) ويستحب للعليل أيضا أن يقول أعوذ بعزة الله وقدرته من شر ما أجد وأحاذر وقال علي بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه إذا شكا أحدكم بطنه فليسأل امرأته شيئا من صداقها ويشتري به عسلا ويشربه بماء السماء فيجتمع له الهنيء والمريء والشفاء", "english": "A man defended him, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Whoever defends the honor of his brother shall have it as a shield for him against the Fire.\" (1)", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 435", "section_title_en": "Section 435", "section_index": 435, "anchors": "- Root: رد (r-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return, repel, defend, avert; al-radd ʿan al-ʿirḍ is to repel defamation from one's honor\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Defense against false accusation; the theological nuance of divine recompense (al-mukāfaʾah) for defending a brother's honor\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal repulsion of physical harm extended metaphorically to verbal defense of honor (ḥaqīqah vs. majāz)\n- Root: عطس (ʿ-t-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sneezing; al-tashmīt is the invocation of mercy upon the sneezer\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sneeze is attributed to God (min Allāh) as a sign of divine favor, while yawning is attributed to Satan\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The response \"yarḥamukallāh\" is a supplication, not an assertion — distinguishing literal invocation from mere congratulation\n- Root: عاد (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To visit repeatedly; al-ʿiyādah is the visitation of the sick\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Returning to someone in his affliction; the reward is measured by the intensity of compassion shown\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The \"return\" (ʿawd) is literal in visitation, but the reward is spiritual — a metaphorical extension of recompense\n- Root: شيع (sh-y-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To accompany, follow; al-tashyīʿ is the funeral procession\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Following the bier with the intention of honoring the deceased and taking admonition (al-iʿtibār)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The qīrāṭ is a measure of reward — metaphorical weight, not literal coin\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The construction \"man ... fa-...\" (whoever does X, then Y) establishes a causal nexus requiring the apodosis to be a new sentence, often carrying the force of divine decree.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "4": [{"arabic": "بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم أعيذك بالله الأحد الصمد الذي لم يلد ولم يولد ولم يكن له كفوا أحد من شر ما تجد قالها مرارا (6) ودخل صلى الله عليه وسلم على علي بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه وهو مريض فقال له قل اللهم إني أسألك تعجيل عافيتك أو صبرا على بليتك أو خروجا من الدنيا إلى رحمتك فإنك ستعطي إحداهن (7) ويستحب للعليل أيضا أن يقول أعوذ بعزة الله وقدرته من شر ما أجد وأحاذر وقال علي بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه إذا شكا أحدكم بطنه فليسأل امرأته شيئا من صداقها ويشتري به عسلا ويشربه بماء السماء فيجتمع له الهنيء والمريء والشفاء P", "english": "A man defended him, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Whoever defends the honor of his brother shall have it as a shield for him against the Fire.\" (1)", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 435", "section_title_en": "Section 435", "section_index": 435, "anchors": "- Root: رد (r-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return, repel, defend, avert; al-radd ʿan al-ʿirḍ is to repel defamation from one's honor\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Defense against false accusation; the theological nuance of divine recompense (al-mukāfaʾah) for defending a brother's honor\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal repulsion of physical harm extended metaphorically to verbal defense of honor (ḥaqīqah vs. majāz)\n- Root: عطس (ʿ-t-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sneezing; al-tashmīt is the invocation of mercy upon the sneezer\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sneeze is attributed to God (min Allāh) as a sign of divine favor, while yawning is attributed to Satan\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The response \"yarḥamukallāh\" is a supplication, not an assertion — distinguishing literal invocation from mere congratulation\n- Root: عاد (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To visit repeatedly; al-ʿiyādah is the visitation of the sick\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Returning to someone in his affliction; the reward is measured by the intensity of compassion shown\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The \"return\" (ʿawd) is literal in visitation, but the reward is spiritual — a metaphorical extension of recompense\n- Root: شيع (sh-y-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To accompany, follow; al-tashyīʿ is the funeral procession\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Following the bier with the intention of honoring the deceased and taking admonition (al-iʿtibār)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The qīrāṭ is a measure of reward — metaphorical weight, not literal coin\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The construction \"man ... fa-...\" (whoever does X, then Y) establishes a causal nexus requiring the apodosis to be a new sentence, often carrying the force of divine decree.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Du'a"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 250, 726], [2, 726, 1246], [3, 1246, 2101], [4, 2101, 2816]], "2": [[1, 250, 1184], [2, 1184, 2503]], "3": [[1, 250, 644], [2, 644, 1382], [3, 1382, 2071], [4, 2071, 3104]], "4": [[1, 350, 1012], [2, 1012, 1674], [3, 1674, 2175], [4, 2175, 3139], [5, 3139, 3763]]}, "minshawi": {"1": [[1, 90, 602], [2, 602, 1370], [3, 1370, 2301], [4, 2301, 3070]], "2": [[1, 10, 1088], [2, 1088, 2580]], "3": [[1, 30, 386], [2, 386, 1223], [3, 1223, 2006], [4, 2006, 3270]], "4": [[1, 10, 956], [2, 956, 1902], [3, 1902, 2472], [4, 2472, 3999], [5, 3999, 4710]]}, "mah": {"1": [[1, 5009, 5310], [2, 5410, 5691], [3, 5751, 6694], [4, 6835, 8521]], "112:1": [[1, 5009, 5310], [2, 5410, 5691], [3, 5751, 6694], [4, 6835, 8521]], "2": [[1, 8541, 9504], [2, 9605, 11150]], "112:2": [[1, 8541, 9504], [2, 9605, 11150]], "3": [[1, 11291, 11592], [2, 11672, 12314], [3, 12415, 12997], [4, 13077, 14703]], "112:3": [[1, 11291, 11592], [2, 11672, 12314], [3, 12415, 12997], [4, 13077, 14703]], "4": [[1, 14783, 15265], [2, 15365, 15907], [3, 16148, 16790], [4, 16991, 17994], [5, 18155, 19339]], "112:4": [[1, 14783, 15265], [2, 15365, 15907], [3, 16148, 16790], [4, 16991, 17994], [5, 18155, 19339]]}, "abdulbasit_warsh": {"112:1": [[1, 861, 1113], [2, 1113, 1512], [3, 1512, 2331], [4, 2331, 3456]], "1": [[1, 861, 1113], [2, 1113, 1512], [3, 1512, 2331], [4, 2331, 3456]], "112:2": [[1, 819, 1533], [2, 1533, 2881]], "2": [[1, 819, 1533], [2, 1533, 2881]], "112:3": [[1, 672, 903], [2, 903, 1470], [3, 1470, 2163], [4, 2163, 3456]], "3": [[1, 672, 903], [2, 903, 1470], [3, 1470, 2163], [4, 2163, 3456]], "112:4": [[1, 588, 1113], [2, 1113, 1785], [3, 1785, 2394], [4, 2394, 3213], [5, 3213, 4109]], "4": [[1, 588, 1113], [2, 1113, 1785], [3, 1785, 2394], [4, 2394, 3213], [5, 3213, 4109]]}}, "letterTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"112:1": [{"wordIdx": 0, "charIdx": 0,